# THE READER BRIDGE — EVERY HALF. DO NOT REMOVE ANY OF THESE ENTRIES.
#
# The two reader-*.html files are GENERATED (`npm run reader:build-html`) and
# tracked. Formatting one by hand puts it out of step with its generator, which CI
# fails on.
#
# The two *.template.html files are the sources, and in them INDENTATION IS
# SEMANTIC — not to the browser, to the drift guard. readerBridge.test.ts reads
# them as TEXT and finds the bridge surface with position-anchored regexes:
# `^\s{10}(\w+): function` for the window.TFReader methods, and an eight-space `};`
# for the end of the object. Prettier reindents embedded <script> content, so a
# single format-on-save silently moves the thing those patterns are anchored to.
#
# The failure is loud but MISLEADING: the guard reports an empty method list and
# reads as "the bridge drifted" when nothing about the bridge changed. Same class
# of trap as the contract canary below.
#
# reader.bridge.html is here for a DIFFERENT reason, so do not "tidy" it away with
# the others: it is a .html file whose contents are raw JavaScript (it is spliced
# into both templates' IIFEs). Prettier would parse it as HTML and mangle it. Its
# indentation is NOT semantic — buildReaderHtml.ts re-indents it on the way in.
#
# `npm run format` cannot reach any of these (its glob is {ts,tsx,js,jsx,json,md} —
# no html). An editor's prettier extension can, because it honours THIS file and
# not that glob. Which is exactly why the entries have to live here.
assets/reader/reader-epub.html
assets/reader/reader-pdf.html
src/features/reader/webview/reader-epub.template.html
src/features/reader/webview/reader-pdf.template.html
src/features/reader/webview/reader.bridge.html

